{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-39189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 10 \\\\ -5 & 12\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "8aa12f4d718ae20a2a827b1c37e8c0d80e5f73860647e57a873d5cc2e1e71adf"} {"question" "q-39189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 10 \\\\ -5 & 12-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(12-\\lambda )-(-5)(10) & =0 \\\\ -24+2\\lambda -12\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+50 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +26 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^{2}-4(1)(26)}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm 2\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =5\\pm \\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5-\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 10 \\\\ -5 & 12\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5-\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7+\\text{i} & 10 \\\\ -5 & 7+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-7+\\text{i}){x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(7+\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5-2\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(7+\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(7+\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}7+\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=5+\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}7-\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}} & =\\frac{(7+\\text{i})(7-\\text{i})}{(5)(5)}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "8aa12f4d718ae20a2a827b1c37e8c0d80e5f73860647e57a873d5cc2e1e71adf"} {"question" "q-39189" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1e38e5244a27b8e449c4c7699c9268f7c5563d29eeda8dac8d75833def743852"} {"question" "q-39189" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "8aa12f4d718ae20a2a827b1c37e8c0d80e5f73860647e57a873d5cc2e1e71adf"} {"question" "q-39189" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{48}{25}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "8aa12f4d718ae20a2a827b1c37e8c0d80e5f73860647e57a873d5cc2e1e71adf"} {"question" "q-39189" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "8aa12f4d718ae20a2a827b1c37e8c0d80e5f73860647e57a873d5cc2e1e71adf"} {"question" "q-81030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "8aa12f4d718ae20a2a827b1c37e8c0d80e5f73860647e57a873d5cc2e1e71adf"} {"question" "q-81030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 13 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1+3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2-3\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 13 & 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 13{x}_{1}+(2-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-1+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-2-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ -(2+3\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2+3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-1}{2+3\\text{i}}=\\frac{-(2-3\\text{i})}{13}=-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "8aa12f4d718ae20a2a827b1c37e8c0d80e5f73860647e57a873d5cc2e1e71adf"} {"question" "q-81030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1e38e5244a27b8e449c4c7699c9268f7c5563d29eeda8dac8d75833def743852"} {"question" "q-81030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "8aa12f4d718ae20a2a827b1c37e8c0d80e5f73860647e57a873d5cc2e1e71adf"} {"question" "q-81030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2-3\\text{i}"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}"] ["math" "2+3\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{13}-\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{3}{5}\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "8aa12f4d718ae20a2a827b1c37e8c0d80e5f73860647e57a873d5cc2e1e71adf"} {"question" "q-81030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2}{13}+\\frac{3}{13}\\text{i}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "8aa12f4d718ae20a2a827b1c37e8c0d80e5f73860647e57a873d5cc2e1e71adf"} {"question" "q-81184" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{5}\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "8aa12f4d718ae20a2a827b1c37e8c0d80e5f73860647e57a873d5cc2e1e71adf"} {"question" "q-81184" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =\\sqrt{5}\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+(-2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}(2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}(2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-3}{2+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}}=\\frac{-3(2-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})}{9}=-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "8aa12f4d718ae20a2a827b1c37e8c0d80e5f73860647e57a873d5cc2e1e71adf"} {"question" "q-81184" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1e38e5244a27b8e449c4c7699c9268f7c5563d29eeda8dac8d75833def743852"} {"question" "q-81184" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "8aa12f4d718ae20a2a827b1c37e8c0d80e5f73860647e57a873d5cc2e1e71adf"} {"question" "q-81184" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "8aa12f4d718ae20a2a827b1c37e8c0d80e5f73860647e57a873d5cc2e1e71adf"} {"question" "q-81184" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\text{i}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "8aa12f4d718ae20a2a827b1c37e8c0d80e5f73860647e57a873d5cc2e1e71adf"} {"question" "q-140385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5-4\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "8aa12f4d718ae20a2a827b1c37e8c0d80e5f73860647e57a873d5cc2e1e71adf"} {"question" "q-140385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =5-4\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 10 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5-4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+4\\text{i} & 10 \\\\ -2 & 2+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-2+4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(2+4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5-4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1+2\\text{i}}{1}=1+2\\text{i}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "8aa12f4d718ae20a2a827b1c37e8c0d80e5f73860647e57a873d5cc2e1e71adf"} {"question" "q-140385" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1e38e5244a27b8e449c4c7699c9268f7c5563d29eeda8dac8d75833def743852"} {"question" "q-140385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "8aa12f4d718ae20a2a827b1c37e8c0d80e5f73860647e57a873d5cc2e1e71adf"} {"question" "q-140385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1-2\\text{i}"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}"] ["math" "1+2\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}+\\text{i}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}-\\text{i}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "8aa12f4d718ae20a2a827b1c37e8c0d80e5f73860647e57a873d5cc2e1e71adf"} {"question" "q-140385" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1+2\\text{i}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "8aa12f4d718ae20a2a827b1c37e8c0d80e5f73860647e57a873d5cc2e1e71adf"} {"question" "q-140388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "8aa12f4d718ae20a2a827b1c37e8c0d80e5f73860647e57a873d5cc2e1e71adf"} {"question" "q-140388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-(4)(-5) & =0 \\\\ 21-7\\lambda -3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-10)\\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^{2}-4(1)(41)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-10)\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5+4\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-4\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -5 & -2-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(2-4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5+4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+4\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=5-4\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2-4\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-5)(-5)}{(2+4\\text{i})(2-4\\text{i})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/state.json" "file_sha256" "8aa12f4d718ae20a2a827b1c37e8c0d80e5f73860647e57a873d5cc2e1e71adf"} {"question" "q-140388" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062195/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1e38e5244a27b8e449c4c7699c9268f7c5563d29eeda8dac8d75833def743852"} {"question" "q-140388" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio